# Limitations

This project's synthetic panel does not replace real user research (see
`README.md`). This file states, from `docs/validity_report.md`'s measured
results, where the panel is useful, where it is not, and what it
systematically misses.

## Where the panel is useful

Reproducibility is real and measured: the same seed, run again, produces the same finding count and the same top-5 cluster membership every time (variance {{ report.reproducibility.finding_count_variance }}, mean top-5 Jaccard overlap {{ report.reproducibility.mean_top5_cluster_jaccard }}). Any disagreement between two runs of the same study configuration is a real signal worth investigating, not measurement noise from the harness itself.

## Where it is not, and what it systematically misses

### 1. Cluster themes partly reflect the Analyst's writing style, not just panel agreement

Near-duplicate paraphrases of one problem cluster at a maximum internal cosine distance of {{ "%.3f"|format(report.clustering_margin.near_duplicate_max_internal_distance) }}; distinct problems phrased in the same uniform "Analyst voice" sit as close as {{ "%.3f"|format(report.clustering_margin.distinct_pair_min_distance) }} apart. The margin between them ({{ "%.3f"|format(report.clustering_margin.margin) }}, against a `distance_threshold` of {{ report.clustering_margin.distance_threshold }}) is real but not large: TF-IDF cosine similarity over Analyst-authored summaries partly measures how uniformly the Analyst writes, not how much the underlying panel actually agrees. A study whose Analyst calls happen to use more uniform phrasing will show tighter, larger clusters than one whose calls don't, independent of what the personas actually reported.

### 2. A rare theme and a broken measurement look identical in the report

Of {{ report.zero_vector_conflation.total_singleton_clusters }} singleton clusters in a targeted fixture, {{ report.zero_vector_conflation.zero_vector_singleton_count }} came from a finding whose summary vectorised to an all-zero TF-IDF row (short or generic enough that every token was excluded before clustering even started), and {{ report.zero_vector_conflation.genuine_frequency_one_singleton_count }} were genuine frequency-1 themes. Nothing in a rendered report tells them apart -- a reader sees "1 persona reported this" either way, whether that means a real, rare finding or a finding the clustering pipeline never had a chance to place.

